# Backend Standards

Technology

Go Fiber v3

PostgreSQL

pgx

sqlc

golang-migrate

Redis

JWT

RBAC

OpenAPI / Swagger

Viper

Zap

Argon2id

---

# Architecture

Always follow:

Handler

↓

Service

↓

Repository

↓

Database

Never bypass layers.

---

# Handler

Handlers should:

Validate input.

Call service.

Return response.

Nothing else.

Never place business logic here.

---

# Service

Service contains:

Business Rules

Validation

Authorization

Transactions

Calculations

---

# Repository

Responsible only for:

Database access via sqlc-generated queries.

No business logic.

---

# DTO

Never expose database rows or internal models directly.

Always use DTOs.

---

# Validation

Validate every request.

Never trust user input.

---

# Error Handling

Return consistent API responses.

Never expose internal errors.

Log internal errors.

Return friendly messages.

---

# Authentication

JWT Access Token (Authorization Bearer)

Refresh Token Rotation (HTTP-only Secure Cookie)

Password Hashing using Argon2id.

---

# Authorization

RBAC with permission checks.

Never check roles inside handlers.

Use middleware.

MVP roles:

Customer

Admin

Super Admin

Visitor is unauthenticated and is not a persisted role.

---

# Logging

Use Zap structured JSON logging.

Log:

Errors

Warnings

Security events

Authentication events

Never log:

Passwords

Tokens

Secrets

---

# Transactions

Use transactions whenever:

Multiple tables are modified.

Transaction boundaries belong in the Service layer.

---

# API Design

RESTful.

Versioned.

/api/v1

Future ready for /api/v2

---

# Database

Database-first.

Write SQL migrations with golang-migrate.

Generate type-safe queries with sqlc.

Never edit generated sqlc code manually.

Avoid ad-hoc SQL outside repository packages.

---

# Configuration

Load configuration with Viper at startup.

Never read environment variables directly inside handlers or services.

---

# Performance

Prevent N+1 queries.

Use indexes.

Paginate large datasets.

---

# Security

Sanitize inputs.

Validate uploads.

Rate limit authentication endpoints.

Protect against SQL injection.

Protect against XSS.

Protect CSRF for cookie-based refresh flows.

---

# Quality Checklist

✓ Layer respected

✓ Validation

✓ Authorization

✓ Logging

✓ Transactions

✓ DTO

✓ Tests

✓ Documentation
